02:43:27.480 --> 02:43:30.250
flock license plate reader system, um.

02:43:31.030 --> 02:43:32.870
The log of the searches run by city users,

02:43:32.990 --> 02:43:36.429
the log of outside agencies searching Huntsville's data and the data sharing

02:43:36.429 --> 02:43:36.900
settings.

02:43:37.150 --> 02:43:40.980
I didn't ask for video, not case files, just the usage logs,

02:43:41.389 --> 02:43:45.870
records of the state's own LPR rules require to exist precisely so the public

02:43:45.870 --> 02:43:48.509
can tell whether a surveillance system is being used properly.

02:43:49.780 --> 02:43:52.750
Over those eight months, the city changed its position 4 times.

02:43:53.179 --> 02:43:57.250
First, a blanket denial, then an estimate of roughly $1 million a number

02:43:57.250 --> 02:44:01.299
they reached by treating a single spreadsheet export as 1.7 million printed

02:44:01.299 --> 02:44:01.839
pages.

02:44:03.719 --> 02:44:06.740
Then I claimed that the electronic version of the files is impossible,

02:44:06.919 --> 02:44:10.080
even though Flock's own published guide walks agents through downloading

02:44:10.080 --> 02:44:13.440
these exact logs as spreadsheets, a guide I sent them.

02:44:14.110 --> 02:44:17.049
And then on August 3rd, I received a final denial.

02:44:17.509 --> 02:44:20.429
The city now says there is no difference between the oversight logs and

02:44:20.429 --> 02:44:23.830
the license plate data itself, and that even though it can produce these

02:44:23.830 --> 02:44:26.099
files electronically, it doesn't have to.

02:44:26.429 --> 02:44:28.790
My only remaining option, they told me, is court.

02:44:29.750 --> 02:44:33.150
Meanwhile, Blount County, Alabama has the same Open Records Act,

02:44:33.469 --> 02:44:37.750
same records, and it gave a complete flog flock audit log to a newspaper

02:44:37.750 --> 02:44:40.650
this summer as one spreadsheet in about 2 months.

02:44:41.070 --> 02:44:44.410
The same thing that Huntsville says would cost me a million dollars.

02:44:45.349 --> 02:44:48.490
That Blount County log also showed exactly why these records matter.

02:44:48.860 --> 02:44:51.429
96% of the searches had no case number attached,

02:44:51.709 --> 02:44:53.809
and nearby in Saint Clair County, audit logs,

02:44:53.980 --> 02:44:57.709
um, are how one officer was terminated and another resigned this summer

02:44:57.709 --> 02:44:59.030
over unauthorized searches.

02:44:59.830 --> 02:45:03.099
In one case, searches of the officer's own family.

02:45:04.870 --> 02:45:08.250
These logs are not anti-police, they are how misuse gets caught.

02:45:08.629 --> 02:45:11.469
The CEEGI standards the city brought up in my meeting with them require

02:45:11.469 --> 02:45:13.049
this logging for exactly that reason.

